      "id": 1,
      "text": "Victoria was founded in 1819 by James Bardin Palmer, an immigrant lawyer and agent for the Earl of Westmoreland.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victoria%2C_Prince_Edward_Island",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Palmer’s Range Light houses the Victoria Seaport Museum, where the Keeper’s of the Light exhibit tells the town’s maritime story.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victoria%2C_Prince_Edward_Island",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "By the late 1800s Victoria thrived with three wharves and many businesses, becoming a key trade hub with Europe and the West Indies.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victoria%2C_Prince_Edward_Island",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Victoria Union rink once hosted one of the Maritimes’ most successful hockey teams, the Victoria Union.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victoria%2C_Prince_Edward_Island",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Today, Victoria supports agriculture, fishing, and an aquaculture research facility, while tourism remains a major part of its economy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20,
